<Project>

  <!--
    TargetFramework is deliberately NOT set here - it is declared per-project.

    Stryker.NET's project analysis (Buildalyzer) fails outright with
    "Failed to analyze project builds" when the TFM comes from Directory.Build.props
    rather than the .csproj; its diagnostic log says only "No analyzer results to log",
    with no MSBuild error pointing at the cause. Bisected 2026-08-07. Every other
    property below is safe to centralize.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>

    <!-- D6 / global standards: explicit usings, non-null by default, zero warnings. -->
    <ImplicitUsings>disable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <WarningLevel>9999</WarningLevel>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!-- Roslyn analyzer stack, enabled at build time rather than IDE-only (D6). -->
    <EnableNETAnalyzers>true</EnableNETAnalyzers>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>

    <!-- **CA1873 is suppressed, and this is a deliberate exception to Zero Warnings (D83).**

         **What the rule is really about**, because a suppression with a vague reason is one that
         gets relaxed later for a bad one: arguments to ILogger are evaluated BEFORE the level is
         consulted. `Log()` is handed finished values and only then asks IsEnabled. So there are two
         costs, and the second is the one that makes it fire on a plain local:

           1. computing the argument - `new FileInfo(path).Length`, `string.Join`, a LINQ count;
           2. the ALLOCATION - LogInformation(string, params object?[]) has no arity-specific
              overloads, so every call allocates an object[] and boxes each int and bool, whether
              or not anything will read the line.

         The bug it exists to catch is debug logging left in a hot path, shipped disabled, still
         paying in full for lines nobody reads.

         **Why it does not apply here - and the first version of this comment got the reason wrong,
         which is worth keeping.** It argued that the sink is always enabled at Information so a
         guard could never save anything. The owner pointed out that this will not stay true: "we
         are going to want to disable most of this logging when we go to production... because we
         are logging like everything". Once a minimum level is configurable, a filtered-out call
         still evaluates its arguments and still allocates the params array - which is precisely the
         waste this rule describes.

         The real reason is about FREQUENCY, not about enablement. A disabled call only costs
         something if it runs often, and almost every log site in this solution is once per load or
         once per event: opening a demo, reading a map, resolving a material set. One small array
         per map load is nothing whether or not anybody reads the line.

         The sites that genuinely run hot - the viewmodel pass, the prop census, the material joins
         - carry hand-written IsEnabled guards, and those are the ones the rule is actually for.
         That split is the whole justification: suppressing the rule is not the same as ignoring
         what it warns about, and a blanket guard everywhere would have made the expensive cases
         indistinguishable from the trivial ones.

         **Revisit this if a per-frame log is ever added.** A new call inside a draw or update path
         needs its own guard, and nothing here will tell you so.

         Satisfying it regardless would mean an `if (log.IsEnabled(...))` around all 193 log calls
         in this solution, or a [LoggerMessage] partial declaration for each. The owner's call,
         2026-08-24: "A is a lot of generated code too, so i think im fine with C".

         **What is NOT suppressed is the case the rule is actually for.** Where an argument is
         genuinely expensive - a filesystem call, `string.Join` over a material set, a LINQ count
         over a collection - the call keeps an explicit `IsEnabled` guard, written by hand and
         commented. Those are in Device3D and WorldRenderer today. Suppressing the rule is not the
         same as ignoring what it is warning about, and the difference is the point: a blanket
         guard everywhere would have made the expensive cases indistinguishable from the trivial
         ones. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1873</NoWarn>

    <!-- **Dependency advisories fail the restore, transitive ones included.**

         Stated explicitly rather than left to the SDK default, which has historically covered
         DIRECT dependencies only - so an advisory against something a package pulls in would not
         have been reported at all. `all` is the mode that walks the whole graph and `low` is the
         floor, because a severity threshold is a decision about which vulnerabilities to ignore
         and this project has no reason to ignore any: it is a few dozen packages, not hundreds.

         Paired with TreatWarningsAsErrors above, which is what turns a new advisory into a failed
         build rather than a line that scrolls past. That pairing is the point - NuGetAudit on its
         own emits warnings, and this repo's whole convention is that a warning is a defect.

         Suggested by the TcgDex.CSharpSdk agent's convention audit (PinKushin/TF2DEMOSALVAGE-LOG.md,
         2026-08-22) and verified absent here before adding. -->
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>

    <!-- **InvariantGlobalization is deliberately NOT set, and this comment exists so it stays
         that way.**

         The same convention audit flagged it as something the SDK sets and this repo must not:
         it strips ICU and culture data, and `docs/memory/international-names-are-required.md`
         records a real defect it would resurrect - a player named `miałker` came through the
         header as `mia??ker` while the userinfo table read it correctly, and both looked like
         plausible names.

         Correct for a REST client with no culture-sensitive string work; wrong for a decoder whose
         inputs are strangers' player names. Written down because the natural way to adopt a
         convention audit is to copy the list, and this is the entry that must not be copied. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>

    <!-- **Unsafe is the project's stated first resort, not its last.** CLAUDE.md is explicit that
         C# comes first for everything including the performance-sensitive parts, and that
         `unsafe`/`Span<T>`/`stackalloc`/`MemoryMarshal` are what to reach for before dropping to
         native code - which cannot be done with the switch off.

         Declared here rather than per project because four of the five managed projects had already
         turned it on individually and the fifth had not, which is how a setting becomes something
         you check rather than something you know. One place, every project, including tests: the UI
         suite needs it for the two user32 calls that ask which process owns the foreground.

         This does NOT weaken the analyzer stack above. Unsafe code is still bound by every rule in
         this file, and CA5392-style pointer and interop rules apply to it specifically. -->
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>

    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <Deterministic>true</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version in Directory.Packages.props, like every other package (D50). -->
    <PackageReference Include="SonarAnalyzer.CSharp">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
